<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zuo Zongtang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1812">1812</a>–<a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zuo Zongtang (1812–1885) was a Chinese statesman and general of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>. He helped suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a>, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou-navy-yard">Fuzhou Navy Yard</a> and crushed the Dungan revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>. From 1876 he led the Qing reconquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-beg">Yaqub Beg</a>'s Yettishar, after which Xinjiang was made a province in 1884.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zuo-zongtang.jpg" alt="Zuo Zongtang" width="320" height="468" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boiarskii, Adolf-Nikolay Erazmovich · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zuo_Zongtang_1875.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zuo Zongtang was born on 10 November 1812 into a land-holding family in Xiangyin County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a>, and mastered the Confucian classics at a private school from the age of five. He failed the imperial examination seven times and withdrew to his home by the Xiang River to farm silkworms and read, and in those years he first turned to Western science. He liked to compare himself with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a>, and his stubbornness earned him the nickname Zuo the mule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a> broke out he became an adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a>, and in 1860 he was given command of 5,000 volunteers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-army">Xiang Army</a>. He drove the rebels into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>, became governor of that province in 1863, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> in 1864 and was made First Class Count Kejing. He, Zeng Guofan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> were remembered together as the leaders who put down the rebellion. As viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> and Zhejiang he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s first modern shipyard and naval academy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a>.</p><p>As viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> he put down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nian-rebellion">Nian Rebellion</a> in 1868 and then marched west with 120,000 men, armed with Dreyse needle rifles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krupp">Krupp</a> artillery, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dungan-revolt">Dungan Revolt</a>. Hui generals who had defected to him, among them Ma Zhan'ao and Ma Anliang, helped him crush the rebels. He suppressed Yakub Beg's uprising in 1878, massed troops toward Kuldja, then occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and won the court debate against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a>, who wanted to abandon what he called useless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>. He was raised to Second Class Marquis Kejing in 1878.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Uneasy with court politics, he asked to leave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> and became viceroy of Liangjiang in 1881. At the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-french-war">Sino-French War</a> he took charge of coastal defence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, and he died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> on 5 September 1885, receiving the posthumous name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenxiang">Wenxiang</a>. In the northwest he had promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> in place of opium, written pamphlets on intensive small-field farming and set up a press that printed the classics. The American dish General Tso's chicken, created by the chef Peng Chang-kuei, was named in his honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zuo Zongtang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Anqing in 1861; in 1865 he and Li Hongzhang founded the Jiangnan Arsenal at Shanghai, with machinery bought in the United States by Yung Wing, and Li founded another arsenal at Nanjing; in 1866 <b>Zuo Zongtang</b> founded the Fuzhou Navy Yard, with French help and a naval school.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Against them stood Zeng Guofan, who recruited, paid and drilled village soldiers for the Xiang Army, <b>Zuo Zongtang</b> and Li Hongzhang, and the Ever Victorious Army of Frederick Townsend Ward and Charles George Gordon.</blockquote><a href="/movement/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qing general <b>Zuo Zongtang</b> reconquered Xinjiang from 1876.</blockquote><a href="/state/yettishar">Yettishar</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Xiangyin County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=28.68,112.88&amp;year=1859" aria-label="Xiangyin County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Zuo Zongtang"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4467 5096h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4467 5096h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=28.68,112.88&amp;year=1859">What was here in 1859? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
